# A small fine-tune against API models, on one narrow task

## The question

Can a LoRA fine-tune of a small open model match API models on a narrow, well-specified task, and what does each cost to run? The task is to turn a request into one JSON function call: an intent (one of {{ dataset.n_intents }}) and slots (each one of {{ dataset.n_slot_types }} types, its value copied from the request). The data is the English part of {{ dataset.name }} {{ dataset.version }}: {{ dataset.n_train | count }} train, {{ dataset.n_dev | count }} dev and {{ dataset.n_test | count }} test requests.

## The setup

The comparison has {{ systems | length }} systems:

{% for s in systems %}
- `{{ s.name }}`: {% if s.name == reference %}{{ base_model.name }} plus the LoRA adapter{% else %}{{ s.model }}{% endif %}, prompt `{{ s.prompt }}` ({{ s.prompt_note }}), {{ s.where }}.
{% endfor %}

The fine-tune is a LoRA adapter (rank {{ training.lora.r }}, alpha {{ training.lora.alpha }}, {{ training.epochs }} epochs{% if training.source != "log" %}, as planned: no training run has finished yet{% endif %}) fitted to the human-labelled train split only; no API output is a label. The API systems run on free tiers with daily request caps, so every system is scored on a fixed, seeded test subset, stratified by scenario:{% for name, info in dataset.subsets.items() if info.split == "test" %} {{ name }} ({{ info.n | count }} items{% if info.parent %}, inside {{ info.parent }}{% endif %}){{ "," if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Each system's configuration must be locked, with a reason, before the runner will touch the test split. {% if has_results %}{{ table_notice }}{% else %}No money is spent on the API systems: their costs are computed at paid list prices.{% endif +%}

