## Cost and break-even

{% if has_results and tables.cost is defined %}
{{ tables.cost }}

{% if gpu %}
A {{ gpu.label }} instance rented around the clock for {{ hours_per_month }} hours at the {{ gpu.price_basis }} price ({{ gpu.usd_per_hour }} an hour) costs {{ gpu.monthly }} a month. Break-even is the monthly volume above which the rental costs less.{% if ft_facts and ft_facts.capacity %} At the operating point ({{ ft_facts.op_concurrency }} concurrent requests, {{ ft_facts.op_rps }} requests/s) one GPU serves {{ ft_facts.capacity }} calls a month{% if ft_facts.p50_1 %}; the fine-tune answers in {{ ft_facts.p50_1 }} at p50 and {{ ft_facts.p95_1 }} at p95 for a single stream, and {{ ft_facts.p95_op }} at p95 under load{% endif %}.{% endif %} Self-hosted cost assumes a GPU kept busy; an idle one costs proportionally more.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
API costs are computed from tokens at the providers' paid list prices, as a no-caching bound and a bound with the static prompt prefix cached. Self-hosted cost comes from throughput measured on Kaggle's free T4, priced at the on-demand rate for renting the same GPU on AWS (g4dn.xlarge). The break-even is the monthly volume at which a GPU rented around the clock ({{ hours_per_month }} hours at the on-demand price) costs less than each API, given that one GPU can serve that volume. The self-hosted figure is the price with the GPU kept fully busy; an idle or half-used GPU costs proportionally more. Self-hosted latency is measured on the box: p50 and p95 for a single stream of requests, and p95 at the operating point. API latency goes to the appendix.
{% endif %}

## Why now

OpenAI's deprecations page ({{ deprecations.url }}, retrieved {{ deprecations.retrieved_on }}) lists these dates for fine-tuned models, evals and new fine-tuning jobs:

{% for event in deprecations.events %}
